    <div class="profile-section-block" id="foundation">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Foundation</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/andrew-ii-of-hungary"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/andrew-ii-of-hungary.jpg" alt="Andrew II of Hungary" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/andrew-ii-of-hungary">Andrew II of Hungary</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Andrew_II_seal_1224.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">György Klösz, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The State of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/teutonic-order">Teutonic Order</a> grew out of a crusading brotherhood founded as a field hospital at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/acre">Acre</a> during the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/third-crusade">Third Crusade</a> in 1190 and reorganised as a military order in 1198. After King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/andrew-ii-of-hungary">Andrew II of Hungary</a> expelled the knights from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/burzenland">Burzenland</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/transylvania">Transylvania</a> in 1225, Duke <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/konrad-i-of-masovia">Konrad I of Masovia</a> invited them to help him against the pagan <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/old-prussians">Prussians</a> who were raiding his lands. Grand Master <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hermann-von-salza">Hermann von Salza</a> secured the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/golden-bull-of-rimini">Golden Bull of Rimini</a>, dated 1226 though possibly issued later, in which Emperor Frederick II granted the Order the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chelmno-land">Kulmerland</a> (<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chelmno">Chełmno</a> Land) and whatever Prussian territory it conquered, and in 1234 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pope-gregory-ix">Pope Gregory IX</a> took these lands under papal protection by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bull-of-rieti">Bull of Rieti</a>. From about 1230 the Landmeister <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hermann-balk">Hermann Balk</a> led the knights across the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vistula">Vistula</a>, founding Thorn in 1231, Kulm in 1232 and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/elblag">Elbing</a> in 1237. In 1237 the Order also absorbed the remnants of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/livonian-brothers-of-the-sword">Livonian Brothers of the Sword</a>, shattered by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lithuanians">Lithuanians</a> at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-saule">Battle of Saule</a>, and so gained a northern branch in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/livonia">Livonia</a>. The conquest of the Prussian tribes took more than fifty years, was interrupted by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/great-prussian-uprising">Great Prussian Uprising</a> of 1260–1274, and ended with the subjugation of the Sudovians around 1283. In 1308 the knights seized <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gdansk">Danzig</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pomerelia">Pomerelia</a>, and in 1309 the Grand Master moved his seat from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/venice">Venice</a> to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/malbork-castle">Marienburg</a>.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="socioeconomy">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Socioeconomy</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/gdansk"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/gdansk.jpg" alt="Gdańsk" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/gdansk">Gdańsk</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Calle_Dlugie_Pobrzeze,_Gdansk,_Polonia,_2013-05-20,_DD_06.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Diego Delso, CC BY-SA 3.0</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The Order ruled a frontier society built by colonisation. Under the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chelmno-law">Kulm charter</a> of 1233, towns received self-government and settlers held land on favourable terms, and from the late thirteenth century thousands of peasants from Silesia, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saxony">Saxony</a> and other German lands founded villages laid out by contractors known as locators. Polish settlers moved into the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chelmno-land">Kulmerland</a> and later into <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/masuria">Masuria</a>, while the surviving <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/old-prussians">Prussians</a>, converted to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/christianity">Christianity</a>, became free tenants or dependent peasants; some Prussian nobles who had sided with the knights kept their status, but the Old Prussian language gradually gave way to German and died out around 1700. The economy rested on grain grown on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vistula">Vistula</a> plain and exported through <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gdansk">Danzig</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/elblag">Elbing</a> to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/flanders">Flanders</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/england">England</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/scandinavia">Scandinavia</a>, alongside <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/timber">timber</a>, potash, wax and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fur-trade">furs</a>. The Order was itself a major trader: its commercial officials, the <em>Großschäffer</em> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/malbork-castle">Marienburg</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/konigsberg">Königsberg</a>, dealt in grain and amber, and it held a monopoly on Baltic amber, much of which was sold to rosary makers in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lubeck">Lübeck</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bruges">Bruges</a>. Danzig, Elbing, Thorn, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chelmno">Kulm</a>, Königsberg and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/braniewo">Braunsberg</a> belonged to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hanseatic-league">Hanseatic League</a>, and the Grand Master was unusual among princes in being treated as a member. Religious life centred on the Catholic Church, organised from 1243 in four Prussian bishoprics.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="military">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Military</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-durbe"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/battle-of-durbe.jpg" alt="Battle of Durbe" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-durbe">Battle of Durbe</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Teutonic_Order_1260.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">S. Bollmann, CC BY-SA 3.0</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The Order's strength lay in a small corps of knight-brothers, numbering in the hundreds rather than thousands in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-prussia">Prussia</a>, supported by serving brothers, levies owed by settlers and Prussian <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vassal">vassals</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mercenary">mercenaries</a> and crusaders from across Catholic <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a>. Its conquest was secured by a dense network of castles, at first of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/timber">timber</a> and earth and later of brick, from Thorn and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kwidzyn">Marienwerder</a> to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/konigsberg">Königsberg</a>, founded in 1255 during a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/crusade">crusade</a> led by King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ottokar-ii-of-bohemia">Ottokar II of Bohemia</a> and named in his honour. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/great-prussian-uprising">Great Prussian Uprising</a>, which followed the defeat of the Order's Livonian branch at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-durbe">Battle of Durbe</a> in 1260, nearly drove the knights out of Prussia before reinforcements restored control. From the early fourteenth century the main enemy was pagan <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lithuania">Lithuania</a>: the Order mounted almost annual raids, the <em>Reisen</em>, into <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/samogitia">Samogitia</a> and Lithuania, joined by noble guests such as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/henry-iv-of-england">Henry Bolingbroke</a>, the future Henry IV of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/england">England</a>, who campaigned with the knights in 1390. In 1370 they defeated the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lithuanians">Lithuanians</a> at the Battle of Rudau, and from 1398 to 1408 they occupied Gotland to suppress piracy. The Order's military reputation collapsed at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-grunwald">Battle of Grunwald</a> (Tannenberg) on 15 July 1410, when a Polish-Lithuanian army under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/w-adys-aw-ii-jagie-o">Władysław II Jagiełło</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vytautas-the-great">Vytautas</a> killed Grand Master <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ulrich-von-jungingen">Ulrich von Jungingen</a> and much of the Order's leadership.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="governance">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Governance &amp; Law</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/heinrich-von-plauen"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/heinrich-von-plauen.jpg" alt="Heinrich von Plauen" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/heinrich-von-plauen">Heinrich von Plauen</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Heinrich_V_von_Plauen.PNG" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Anonymous plate, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The state was ruled by a religious corporation rather than a dynasty. The Grand Master, elected for life by the brethren, governed with a small council of great officers: the Grand Commander, the Marshal, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/knights-hospitaller">Hospitaller</a>, the Drapier and the Treasurer. The general chapter of the Order could in theory depose him, as happened to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/heinrich-von-plauen">Heinrich von Plauen</a> in 1413. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-prussia">Prussia</a> was divided into <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/commandery">commanderies</a>, each run from a castle by a <em><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/komtur">Komtur</a></em> with a convent of brothers, who collected rents, dispensed justice and mustered troops; the Livonian branch had its own master, and the Order's estates in the Empire were run by the German Master. In 1243 the papal legate <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/william-of-modena">William of Modena</a> divided Prussia into the bishoprics of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chelmno">Kulm</a>, Pomesania, Ermland and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sambia-peninsula">Samland</a>, each <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bishop">bishop</a> ruling a third of his diocese as a territory; three of the cathedral chapters were incorporated into the Order, while Ermland kept more independence. Written records were kept with unusual care, and the Order's archive is among the richest of any medieval state. Because the brothers were celibate and recruited mostly from the German lands of the Empire, the native nobility and the towns had no share in government. In 1440 they formed the Prussian Confederation to defend their privileges, a grievance that in 1454 turned into open revolt.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="impact">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Global &amp; Regional Impact</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/malbork-castle"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/malbork-castle.jpg" alt="Malbork Castle" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/malbork-castle">Malbork Castle</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Zesp%C3%B3%C5%82_Zamku_Krzy%C5%BCackiego_MALBORK_01.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Gregy, CC BY-SA 3.0 pl</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The Order's state permanently changed the map and population of the south-eastern Baltic. Its conquest destroyed the independent Prussian tribes and, through settlement, made <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-prussia">Prussia</a> a largely German-speaking land, while the towns it founded, including Thorn, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/elblag">Elbing</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/konigsberg">Königsberg</a>, became major centres of Hanseatic trade. Its brick Gothic architecture, above all the castle of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/malbork-castle">Marienburg</a> (<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/malbork">Malbork</a>), which grew into one of the largest brick fortresses in the world and is now a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/unesco-world-heritage-site">UNESCO World Heritage Site</a>, shaped building across the region. The Order produced a historical literature of its own, notably <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peter-of-dusburg">Peter of Dusburg</a>'s <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/latin">Latin</a> <em><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chronicon-terrae-prussiae">Chronicle of the Prussian Land</a></em>, completed in 1326 and put into German verse by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nicolaus-von-jeroschin">Nicolaus von Jeroschin</a>. Its <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/crusade">crusades</a> against <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lithuania">Lithuania</a> helped push Lithuania into union with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/poland">Poland</a> in 1385, creating the dynastic power that would defeat it. When <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/albert-duke-of-prussia">Albert of Brandenburg-Ansbach</a> secularised the remaining territory in 1525, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/duchy-of-prussia">Duchy of Prussia</a> became the first Lutheran state, and its inheritance by the Hohenzollern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/prince-elector">electors</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brandenburg">Brandenburg</a> in 1618 laid the foundation of the later Kingdom of Prussia. The Order's memory was later used in rival national mythologies: <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/henryk-sienkiewicz">Henryk Sienkiewicz</a>'s novel <em>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/teutonic-order">Teutonic Knights</a></em> cast the knights as aggressors, while German nationalists and later the Nazis claimed them as forerunners of eastward expansion.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="decline">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Decline &amp; Fall</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-grunwald"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/battle-of-grunwald.jpg" alt="Battle of Grunwald" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-grunwald">Battle of Grunwald</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Jan_Matejko,_Bitwa_pod_Grunwaldem.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Jan Matejko, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Defeat at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-grunwald">Grunwald</a> in 1410 did not destroy the state. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/heinrich-von-plauen">Heinrich von Plauen</a> held <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/malbork-castle">Marienburg</a> through a two-month siege, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/first-peace-of-thorn">First Peace of Thorn</a> in 1411 cost the Order little territory beyond <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/samogitia">Samogitia</a>, ceded for the lifetimes of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/w-adys-aw-ii-jagie-o">Jagiełło</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vytautas-the-great">Vytautas</a>, but imposed a heavy indemnity. Ransoms, war taxes and further wars with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/poland">Poland</a>, ended by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/treaty-of-melno">Treaty of Melno</a> in 1422, which fixed the Lithuanian border, drained its finances and alienated its subjects. In 1454 the Prussian Confederation renounced the Order's rule and placed itself under King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/casimir-iv-jagiellon">Casimir IV of Poland</a>, starting the Thirteen Years' War. Unpaid <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mercenary">mercenaries</a> sold Marienburg to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/poles">Poles</a> in 1457, and the Grand Master moved to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/konigsberg">Königsberg</a>. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/second-peace-of-thorn">Second Peace of Thorn</a> in 1466 handed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pomerelia">Pomerelia</a>, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chelmno-land">Kulmerland</a>, Marienburg, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/elblag">Elbing</a> and Ermland to Poland as Royal <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-prussia">Prussia</a>, while the Order kept eastern Prussia as a fief, its Grand Master obliged to swear allegiance to the Polish king. Later Grand Masters, chosen from German princely houses in the hope of imperial help, refused homage. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/albert-duke-of-prussia">Albert of Brandenburg-Ansbach</a>, elected in 1511, fought a last war against Poland in 1519–1521, then followed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/martin-luther">Martin Luther</a>'s advice to secularise. By the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/treaty-of-krakow">Treaty of Kraków</a> he became hereditary Duke of Prussia, paying homage to Sigismund I on 10 April 1525. The Order survived in the Empire and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/livonia">Livonia</a>, but its Prussian state was gone.</p></div>
      </div>

    <p class="profile-summary" id="summary"><em>The State of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/teutonic-order">Teutonic Order</a> turned a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/crusade">crusade</a> against the pagan <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/old-prussians">Prussians</a> into one of the best-administered territories of medieval <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a>, colonising the south-eastern Baltic with German settlers and Hanseatic towns until defeat by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/poland">Poland</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lithuania">Lithuania</a> reduced it and its last Grand Master made it the Lutheran <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/duchy-of-prussia">Duchy of Prussia</a>.</em></p>

